---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import EmailCapture from '../../../components/EmailCapture.astro';
import AccGroup from '../../../components/AccGroup.astro';
import RelatedEquipment from '../../../components/RelatedEquipment.astro';
import { SL_1200MK2, SL_1200MK7 } from '../../../data/facts.js';

// SECOND TECHNICS MODEL PAGE, and the oldest product this site documents by
// nearly forty years. Structure and section order match the sibling,
// src/pages/knowledge/technics/sl-1200mk7.astro, so the two decks read as one
// family and a DJ moving between them finds the same answer in the same place.
//
// WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS AT ALL, given the deck has been out of production for
// well over a decade. It is in far more booths than the MK7. A DJ walking into a
// club is much more likely to be handed one of these, and the document Panasonic
// wrote for it HAS NO TROUBLESHOOTING SECTION. Ten pages, read end to end, and
// there is no such heading anywhere. That is the same argument the Allen & Heath
// pages rest on and it is stronger here.
//
// THE SOURCE IS A SCAN WITH NO TEXT LAYER. Panasonic's own hosted PDF is images
// of a 1979 document, so it was read page by page rather than searched. Worth
// recording because it explains why some fields on this page are thinner than
// their MK7 equivalents: the document genuinely says less, and where it is silent
// this page says so instead of borrowing the modern deck's answer.
//
// THE THREE PLACES THIS PAGE IS CAREFUL, and each one is a place the internet is
// confident and Panasonic is not:
//
// 1. WHEN PRODUCTION ENDED. Everybody says 2010. No Panasonic or Technics
//    document says anything: their own heritage timeline steps from the MK5 era
//    straight to the 2015 revival. So this page gives no year, the same way the
//    DJM-900NXS page refuses to state an end-of-support date nobody published.
//
// 2. WHETHER THE PHONO LEADS DETACH. The manual never uses the words hard-wired.
//    What it does is draw the leads as cables already coming out of the deck, and
//    omit them from a parts checklist that lists every other supplied item down
//    to the 45 rpm adaptor. Two pieces of evidence pointing the same way is an
//    inference, not a quote, and the page says "reads as" rather than "is".
//
// 3. THE SL-1210MK2. No Panasonic document for it could be found at all. The
//    black export twin certainly existed and was sold; every specification hit
//    for it is a third-party archive. Panasonic DO document the silver-and-black
//    naming convention, but only for the modern Grand Class line, and extending
//    that backward forty years would be this site inventing a fact.
//
// PLATTER WEIGHTS ARE NOT COMPARED WITH THE MK7 ANYWHERE ON EITHER PAGE. This
// deck's 2 kg is the platter. The MK7's approximately 1.8 kg includes the slipmat
// and slip sheet. Different measurements, and the feel of a deck is exactly the
// kind of thing a DJ would act on.
const UPDATED = SL_1200MK2.checked;

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/knowledge/technics/sl-1200mk2#article',
  headline: 'SL-1200MK2: the operational reference',
  description: 'What every DJ should know before working a Technics SL-1200MK2: the ground wire Panasonic warn about in capital letters, what silences one channel on a deck whose leads do not unplug, finding zero on a pitch fader with no reset, and the troubleshooting section the manual never had.',
  author: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  datePublished: '2026-08-08',
  dateModified: '2026-08-08',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/knowledge/technics/sl-1200mk2#webpage' },
  image: 'https://www.savemygig.com/images/sl-1200mk2-panel-full.webp',
};
---
<Base
  title="Technics SL-1200MK2: Hum, Pitch, Wiring | Save My Gig"
  description="The SL-1200MK2 field manual: the ground wire Panasonic warn about, what silences one channel, and finding zero on a fader with no reset."
  path="/knowledge/technics/sl-1200mk2"
  crumbName="SL-1200MK2"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />

  <main class="wrap wrap-wide">
    <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/knowledge">Knowledge base</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/knowledge/technics">Technics</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span>SL-1200MK2</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="hw-head">
      <div class="hw-head-text">
        <div class="status-line sl-date">Reviewed: {UPDATED}</div>
        <h1 class="rb-h1">SL-1200MK2, <span class="accent">the field manual</span></h1>
      </div>
      <a
        class="hw-thumb"
        href="#sl-1200mk2-lightbox"
        aria-label="Open a larger photo of the Technics SL-1200MK2 control surface"
      >
        <img
          src="/images/sl-1200mk2-panel-thumb.webp"
          alt="Technics SL-1200MK2 quartz direct drive DJ turntable, top view"
          width="606"
          height="500"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </a>
    </div>

    <div class="lightbox" id="sl-1200mk2-lightbox">
      <a href="#" class="lightbox-backdrop" aria-label="Close"></a>
      <a href="#" class="lightbox-close" aria-label="Close">&times;</a>
      <img
        src="/images/sl-1200mk2-panel-full.webp"
        srcset="/images/sl-1200mk2-panel-800.webp 800w, /images/sl-1200mk2-panel-full.webp 1400w"
        sizes="92vw"
        alt="Technics SL-1200MK2 quartz direct drive DJ turntable, top view"
        width="1400"
        height="1155"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="panel panel-go">
      <input type="checkbox" id="sv-toggle" class="sv-toggle sr-only" />
      <p class="sv-text" style="margin:0">
        <strong>Booth briefing.</strong> The deck most likely to be already bolted
        into the booth, in production from {SL_1200MK2.released}, and its manual
        has no troubleshooting section at all. Hum is the ground wire, and
        Panasonic put HUM in capitals warning you about it. A dead channel is four
        headshell wires or the cartridge, and on this deck the phono leads do not
        unplug, so a damaged lead is a repair rather than a swap. Pitch is
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.pitchRange} with quartz lock and no reset button, so zero
        is found by eye against the platter markings.
      </p>
      <label for="sv-toggle" class="sv-more">Read more…</label>
      <label for="sv-toggle" class="sv-more sv-more-open">Read less</label>
    </div>

    <AccGroup icon="zap" title="Emergency fixes" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="mk2-acc" id="hum"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">There is a hum in the system and it will not go away</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> The ground wire. Panasonic thought this
        was important enough to put the word HUM in capital letters and quotation
        marks, in 1979, in a ten page manual with no troubleshooting section:
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.earthWarning} That is the whole diagnosis and it is the
        first thing to check every time.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> A phono cartridge puts out a few
        millivolts, and the mixer applies something in the region of sixty decibels
        of gain to make it usable. It applies that gain to everything on the input,
        including mains-frequency noise the chassis has picked up. The ground wire
        gives that noise a path out. Without it the chassis floats and you are
        listening to the mains being amplified alongside the record, which is why
        turning things down does not help: the hum and the music are going through
        the same gain stage.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Find the spade lug on the ground wire and
        get it onto the mixer's ground post, screwed down rather than resting
        against it. Panasonic's own wording is that a connection which is missing
        OR LOOSE produces the hum, so a lug that is merely touching is a fault. If
        the ground is definitely good, move the deck and its cables away from other
        equipment and from mains leads before you start suspecting the deck: the
        MK7 manual, which does have a troubleshooting section, names proximity to
        other appliances and their power cords as the second cause, and the physics
        is identical on this deck.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> On a deck that has been in a booth
        for twenty years the ground wire is the part most likely to have been cut
        off, taped up or simply lost. Check for its presence during setup. If it is
        gone, that is a repair to schedule rather than something to work around,
        because there is no other path for the noise.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#dead-channel">One channel has gone silent</a> <a href="#connections">Connections, and what does not unplug</a> <a href={SL_1200MK2.source} rel="noopener">Panasonic's SL-1200MK2 operating instructions</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="mk2-acc" id="dead-channel"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">One channel has gone silent and the other one is fine</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> The headshell wires first, then the
        cartridge. On this deck that is the whole list you can act on in a booth,
        because the phono leads do not come off. Panasonic print the colour code so
        you can see which side you have lost: {SL_1200MK2.headshellWiring}.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> Those four wires are the thinnest
        connection in the chain and they are worked every time somebody changes a
        cartridge or drops a headshell. A wire off its pin, or a pin no longer
        gripping, silences exactly one channel and leaves the other perfect. A
        cartridge that has lost one coil presents identically. Both are mechanical
        and neither shows up anywhere on the top panel, which is why this fault
        sends people into a mixer's routing menu.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Swap the headshell for a known good one.
        That single move tests the wires and the cartridge together and takes ten
        seconds. If the fault follows the headshell it is the wires or the
        cartridge. If the fault stays with the deck, move the deck's leads to a
        different channel on the mixer to find out whether you are chasing a deck
        or a mixer input, and if it is still the deck, you are into the lead itself,
        which on this model is not a booth job.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Carry a second headshell, already
        wired and already balanced. It is the single most useful spare for a vinyl
        set on any deck, and on this one it matters more, because the other end of
        the signal path is not something you can swap.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#hum">There is a hum in the system</a> <a href="#tonearm">Setting the arm up so it tracks</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="mk2-acc" id="skipping"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The needle jumps every time the bass drops</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> This is the room, not the deck, and
        Panasonic's placement instructions are the fix:
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.placementSpeakers} {SL_1200MK2.placementLevel} Then check
        the counterweight and the anti-skating, and only then start suspecting the
        deck.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> A stylus in a groove is a microphone
        and it cannot tell the difference between vibration cut into the record and
        vibration arriving up through the plinth from a subwoofer. Past a certain
        level the plinth, platter and arm move with the room and the stylus climbs
        out. The tell is that it happens on the loud parts and stops when you pull
        the fader down. Panasonic built real isolation into this deck and said so:
        the cast aluminium base, the heavy rubber underside and four insulating
        feet are described in the manual as making it suitable for use at extra
        high sound pressure levels. That is a real design claim, and it is also
        not a substitute for keeping the deck away from the bass bins.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Decouple the deck from whatever is
        resonating and move it away from the front of a speaker. Confirm it is
        level, because an unlevel deck adds a constant sideways pull and skips in
        one direction. Check the counterweight has not crept, and set anti-skating
        to Panasonic's own instruction: {SL_1200MK2.antiSkatingInstruction} Adding
        tracking force is the last resort and it must stay inside whatever your
        cartridge maker specifies.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Judgement from working booths
        rather than a Panasonic specification, said plainly: a booth built with the
        turntables sharing structure with the bass cabinets will always fight you,
        and no arm setup fixes a structural problem. Decide where the decks sit
        before the room fills up, because after that nothing moves.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#tonearm">Setting the arm up so it tracks</a> <a href="#pitch">Finding zero on a fader with no reset</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="mk2-acc" id="pitch"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Finding zero on a fader with no reset button</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> There is no reset on this deck. The
        fader runs {SL_1200MK2.pitchRange} and you find rated speed by eye, against
        the markings Panasonic cut into the platter edge:
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.platterMarkings}. The stroboscope is not decoration, it is
        the instrument.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this matters.</strong> Quartz lock is the headline feature of
        this deck and it is often misread. It means the rotation is
        quartz-referenced and accurate, not that the fader snaps to zero. So a deck
        sitting at half a per cent fast holds that half per cent perfectly all
        night, and nothing on the panel objects. A DJ arriving from an MK7, which
        does have a reset button beside the fader, will look for one here and not
        find it.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Set the fader against the zero marking and
        use the strobe dots to confirm the platter is locked rather than drifting.
        If a record is playing at the wrong speed, check the speed selector before
        the fader: this deck runs {SL_1200MK2.speeds} and nothing else, so a 45
        played at 33 is the more likely mistake here than on a deck with a third
        speed hidden behind a switch.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Make the zero check part of setup
        rather than something you notice in the first mix. It takes a second, and on
        a deck with no reset it is the only time you will get a clean reference
        without a record moving.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#quartz">Quartz lock, and what it actually promises</a> <a href="/knowledge/technics/sl-1200mk7">What changed on the MK7</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="warn" title="Known issues" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="mk2-acc" id="no-troubleshooting"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The manual has no troubleshooting section</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Correct, and it is a fact about the
        document rather than a gap in this page. All ten pages of Panasonic's
        operating instructions were read end to end. There is no troubleshooting
        heading anywhere in it. The MK7 manual, forty years later, does have one.</p>
        <p><strong>What the manual does contain.</strong> The one diagnostic
        sentence in the whole document is the ground wire warning, and it is
        precise: {SL_1200MK2.earthWarning} Everything else useful is in the
        placement and connection instructions rather than under a fault heading,
        which is exactly why nobody finds it.</p>
        <p><strong>What that means for a booth.</strong> The knowledge about this
        deck lives in the hands of people who have worked on it, and almost none of
        it is written down by the manufacturer. There is more unsourced folklore
        about the SL-1200 than about any other piece of DJ equipment ever made. So
        on this page, anything Panasonic wrote down is quoted, and anything that is
        field practice is labelled as field practice. Where they are silent, this
        page says they are silent rather than filling the space.</p>
        <p><strong>What is not written down anywhere.</strong> Panasonic never
        published when production of this deck ended. Their own heritage timeline
        goes from the MK5 era to the 2015 revival with no end date in between. The
        year everyone repeats may well be right, and it is not in a document, so it
        is not on this page.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#specs">What Panasonic publishes, and what they do not</a> <a href={SL_1200MK2.sourceHeritage} rel="noopener">The Technics heritage timeline</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="mk2-acc" id="quartz"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Quartz lock, and what it actually promises</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> It promises accurate, stable rotation
        referenced to a quartz crystal. It does not promise that your fader is at
        zero. Those get conflated constantly and the difference matters in a
        booth.</p>
        <p><strong>Panasonic's own framing.</strong> They describe the pitch as
        variable by up to approximately eight per cent under total quartz-locked
        control. So the quartz reference is what holds whatever speed you have
        selected, including a speed that is not the rated one. Accuracy and
        correctness are two different properties and this deck guarantees the
        first.</p>
        <p><strong>Why the numbers are worth reading properly.</strong> Wow and
        flutter on this deck is published three times over, to three different
        standards: {SL_1200MK2.wowFlutter}. Quoting the smallest of those next to a
        modern single figure compares two different measurements, which is how
        specification arguments about this deck usually start. Rumble is published
        as {SL_1200MK2.rumble}, and notably the MK7 does not publish a rumble
        figure at all.</p>
        <p><strong>The practical version.</strong> Set it against the strobe, trust
        it to hold, and check it again if the deck has been transported. What
        quartz lock buys you is that once it is right it stays right, which is not
        nothing on a deck this old.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#pitch">Finding zero on a fader with no reset</a> <a href="#specs">What Panasonic publishes</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="sliders" title="Features explained" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="mk2-acc" id="features"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">What this deck has, and what it deliberately does not</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>What it has.</strong> {SL_1200MK2.type}, with
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.braking.toLowerCase()}. Quartz-locked pitch across
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.pitchRange}, with platter markings at
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.platterMarkings}. Starting torque
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.startingTorque} and a start-up of
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.startUpTime}, which is the number that made this deck a DJ
        tool rather than a hi-fi turntable.</p>
        <p><strong>What it does not have, and the MK7 does.</strong> No pitch reset
        button. One pitch range rather than two. No reverse play. No adjustable
        starting torque or brake strength. Two speeds rather than three. No
        detachable phono lead, earth lead or power cord. And no stylus light of the
        pop-up kind the MK7 carries.</p>
        <p><strong>Why that list is short on purpose.</strong> Technics themselves
        describe the MK7 as a revival of this deck, and the press release for it
        says it follows the earlier generation on dimensions, button layout and the
        inertial mass of the platter. Almost everything that makes an SL-1200 feel
        like an SL-1200 was set here, in {SL_1200MK2.released}, and has not been
        changed since.</p>
        <p><strong>The isolation, which is a real design claim.</strong> The
        manual describes the cast aluminium base, the heavy rubber underside, the
        damped platter and four large insulating feet, and states the result is
        suited to use at extra high sound pressure levels. That is Panasonic's own
        wording from 1979 and it is why these decks survive in clubs.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="/knowledge/technics/sl-1200mk7">The SL-1200MK7</a> <a href="#skipping">The needle jumps when the bass drops</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="star" title="Setup and calibration" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="mk2-acc" id="tonearm"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Setting the arm up so it tracks</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>The numbers Panasonic publish.</strong> The arm is
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.tonearm.toLowerCase()}, effective length
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.tonearmEffectiveLength}, effective mass
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.tonearmEffectiveMass}, overhang {SL_1200MK2.overhang},
        offset angle {SL_1200MK2.offsetAngle}. Arm height adjusts across
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.armHeightRange}. Stylus pressure dials
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.stylusPressureRange}. Supported cartridge weight is
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.cartridgeWeightRange}, and the headshell weighs
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.headshellWeight}.</p>
        <p><strong>The narrower pressure range is worth noticing.</strong> This arm
        dials {SL_1200MK2.stylusPressureRange}. The MK7's dials
        {' '}{SL_1200MK7.stylusPressureRange}. If you run a cartridge that wants a
        heavier tracking force than this arm can reach directly, that is a real
        constraint on this deck rather than a setup mistake, and Panasonic supply
        auxiliary and shell weights precisely to shift the usable range.</p>
        <p><strong>Anti-skating.</strong> Panasonic's instruction is one line:
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.antiSkatingInstruction} So whatever tracking force your
        cartridge calls for, the anti-skating control goes to the matching value.
        It is the step people skip, and it shows up as a deck that skips in one
        direction rather than randomly.</p>
        <p><strong>The number this page will not give you.</strong> Panasonic
        publish the range the arm can dial and no recommendation for what to set it
        to. Your cartridge maker publishes a tracking force for your specific
        cartridge, and that is the number to use. Anyone quoting one figure for all
        DJ use is guessing on your behalf: too light mistracks and skips, too heavy
        wears records faster.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#skipping">The needle jumps when the bass drops</a> <a href="#maintenance">What wears out</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="usb" title="Connections" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="mk2-acc" id="connections"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Connections, and what does not unplug</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>What comes out of the deck.</strong> {SL_1200MK2.output}. The
        ground wire is a separate lead with a spade lug that screws to the mixer,
        and it is the one described under the hum warning above.</p>
        <p><strong>The leads read as fixed to the deck, and here is the
        evidence.</strong> Panasonic never use the words hard-wired. What they do
        is draw the phono leads in the connection figure as cables already coming
        out of the unit, and leave them out of a parts checklist that lists every
        other supplied item down to the 45 rpm adaptor. Two pieces of evidence
        pointing the same way, and no instruction anywhere for detaching them at
        the deck. So this page says the leads read as fixed rather than stating it
        as a quoted specification, because that is the honest strength of the
        claim.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters in a booth.</strong> If it is fixed, a crushed or
        damaged lead is a soldering job rather than a swap, so the failure that
        takes thirty seconds on an MK7 takes this deck out of service. That is the
        single biggest practical difference between the two, and it is why the MK7
        listing its phono lead as a replaceable part number matters more than it
        sounds.</p>
        <p><strong>Power.</strong> {SL_1200MK2.voltage}, drawing
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.powerConsumption}. The deck weighs
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.weight} and measures {SL_1200MK2.dimensions}, which is
        worth knowing before you agree to carry a pair up a flight of stairs.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#hum">There is a hum in the system</a> <a href="/knowledge/technics/sl-1200mk7">The MK7, where these leads detach</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="sync" title="Compatibility" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="mk2-acc" id="compatibility"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">What it works with, and what it needs from the mixer</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>The mixer has to have a phono input.</strong> That is the whole
        requirement and it is the one people get caught by. A cartridge output
        needs a phono stage, meaning a large amount of gain and RIAA equalisation.
        Into a line input you get a thin, quiet signal with no bass, which sounds
        like a broken deck and is a routing mistake. Every mixer this site
        documents beside it has switchable phono inputs.</p>
        <p><strong>Timecode and DVS.</strong> This is a normal turntable, so
        timecode vinyl works on it the way it works on any turntable and the
        software decides what is supported. Worth knowing that a worn timecode
        record degrades tracking well before it sounds obviously bad, and the
        symptom is software stuttering or losing position rather than audible
        damage.</p>
        <p><strong>Speeds.</strong> {SL_1200MK2.speeds}. No third speed on this
        generation.</p>
        <p><strong>Cartridges.</strong> Supported weight is
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.cartridgeWeightRange} as standard, and Panasonic supply an
        auxiliary weight and a shell weight to shift that range in both directions.
        The wire colour code is {SL_1200MK2.headshellWiring}.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#dead-channel">One channel has gone silent</a> <a href="/knowledge/dictionary">The DJ technical dictionary</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="shield" title="Maintenance" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="mk2-acc" id="maintenance"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">What wears out on a deck this old</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>The stylus is the consumable.</strong> It is the only part in
        contact with the record and it wears from the first play. A worn stylus
        does not announce itself: it mistracks, it makes records sound duller than
        they are, and it damages what it plays. Panasonic publish no life figure
        because it belongs to the cartridge, so use the cartridge maker's
        number.</p>
        <p><strong>What ages on this deck specifically.</strong> Everything that is
        rubber. The mat, the feet and the damping under the platter are the parts
        that have been sitting in a warm booth since before most of the people
        using it were born, and hardened feet are a real cause of the skipping this
        page has a section about. This is field observation rather than a Panasonic
        statement, and it is flagged as such.</p>
        <p><strong>What does not wear.</strong> There is no belt, because it is
        direct drive. There is no firmware to degrade.
        The motor is {SL_1200MK2.motor.toLowerCase()}, a design with no brushes to
        replace, and it is most of why decks from
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.released} are still working nightly.</p>
        <p><strong>Between sets.</strong> Keep the stylus clean, keep the ground
        wire attached, and keep the arm rest clip actually clipped when the deck is
        moved. Most tonearm damage happens in transport rather than in use.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#tonearm">Setting the arm up so it tracks</a> <a href="#skipping">The needle jumps when the bass drops</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="book" title="Understanding the deck" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="mk2-acc" id="specs"><summary><h3 class="acc-h2">What Panasonic publishes, and what they do not</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>The published figures.</strong> {SL_1200MK2.type}. Motor
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.motor.toLowerCase()}. Platter {SL_1200MK2.platter}.
        Speeds {SL_1200MK2.speeds}. Starting torque
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.startingTorque}. Start-up {SL_1200MK2.startUpTime}. Wow
        and flutter {SL_1200MK2.wowFlutter}. Rumble {SL_1200MK2.rumble}. Weight
        {' '}{SL_1200MK2.weight}. Introduced {SL_1200MK2.released}.</p>
        <p><strong>No end-of-production date exists.</strong> Panasonic and
        Technics never published one. Their own heritage timeline steps from the
        MK5 era to the 2015 revival with nothing in between, so the year that gets
        repeated everywhere is not in any document this site could find. It may
        well be right. It is not sourced, so it is not stated here.</p>
        <p><strong>There is no Panasonic document for the SL-1210MK2.</strong> The
        black export twin was certainly made and sold, and every specification or
        manual hit for it is a third-party archive. Panasonic DO document the
        silver-and-black naming convention, but only for the modern Grand Class
        line, and reaching back forty years with it would be this site inventing a
        fact rather than reporting one.</p>
        <p><strong>The source is a scan.</strong> Panasonic's own hosted PDF is
        page images of a 1979 document with no text layer, so it was read page by
        page rather than searched. That is why some of this page is thinner than
        its MK7 equivalent: the document says less.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href={SL_1200MK2.source} rel="noopener">The operating instructions</a> <a href={SL_1200MK2.sourceHistory} rel="noopener">Technics on the history of the SL-1200</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

